            <ArticleTitle>Intra-articular synovial hemangioma; a rare cause of knee pain and swelling.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>571-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Synovial hemangioma is a rare intra-articular benign tumor that is difficult to diagnose because of its nonspecific symptoms. Patients are generally children or young adults with a nontraumatic recurrent swollen painful knee.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE PRESENTATION" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 24-year-old woman presented with a 20-year history of recurrent pain and swelling in the right knee. Physical examination revealed a small painful point adjacent to the medial side of the patella. Magnetic resonance imaging revealed an intra-articular tumor. After arthroscopic examination, surgical excision was performed. The histology was characteristic of synovial hemangioma.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Open complete synovectomy of the circumscribed capillary hemangioma was successful, and the patient remains asymptomatic 2 years after surgery.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
